; Drive build, HDDSYNTH.INI `isa = on`: releases IORDY when ide_bus has held
; it low for about 23 us. ISA IDE cards select the drive from the address
; alone, so a memory cycle or DRAM refresh whose address aliases 1F0h-1F7h
; selects it too, and pass IORDY to IOCHRDY: ide_bus pulls IORDY low at the
; chip select (as ATA's timing needs) and, with no strobe to end the access,
; the cycle would stall for good (an ICL 386 froze, 0.21.0). A real access
; never holds IORDY that long (core 1 answers within about 1 us), and the
; longest gap the ICL's BIOS left between a status read and its data, with the
; drive still selected, was 11 us. Once released, IORDY stays released until
; ide_bus pulls it low again (its next `arm` or `read`): a stalled cycle ends
; however slowly the host's pull-up raises the line, which a 60 ns release
; (0.21.2) didn't allow.
;
; Runs in ide_bus's PIO block (iordy_watchdog.cpp), so both write the same pin
; direction register: this program only ever writes 0 (release).
;
; Pin mapping:
;   JMP_PIN  = GPIO28 (IORDY): high once released, by either program
;   SET      = GPIO28, as a pin direction
;   wait 0 gpio 28: ide_bus has pulled it low
;
; Timing: 16 x 32 passes of 9 cycles, 4,608 cycles: 23 us at 200 MHz
; (host/tests/test_iordy_watchdog_sim.py).

.pio_version 1
.program iordy_watchdog
.wrap_target
top:
    wait 0 gpio 28                  ; ide_bus has pulled IORDY low
    set x, 15
outer:
    set y, 31
hold:
    jmp pin top                     ; released meanwhile: start over
    jmp y-- hold            [7]
    jmp x-- outer
    set pindirs, 0                  ; held too long: release it
.wrap
